{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (i.e., winning) compared to unfavorable ones (i.e., losing). A lottery is left-skewed if the probability mass is concentrated on the higher (favorable) outcomes. In this case, both lotteries have a non-zero probability of winning (2500 or 2400 Euros), and the rest of the probability (67% or 66%) is assigned to 0 Euros. To assess skewness: a lottery is more left-skewed if the probability of the positive outcome is higher relative to the zero outcome. Lottery A has a 33% chance of winning 2500 Euros, and Lottery B has a 34% chance of winning 2400 Euros. Since 34% > 33%, Lottery B has a slightly higher probability of a positive outcome. Thus, Lottery B is more left-skewed than Lottery A. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which in this case is Lottery B. Therefore, the decision reason is applicable and can be evaluated based on the given probabilities and outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The individual states that 'die summen die es zu gewinnen gab hatten meiner meinung nach keinen großen unterscheid' (the sums to be won were, in my opinion, not very different), indicating that the monetary values (2500 vs 2400) were not seen as significantly different. The individual then says 'so habe ich einfach auf die höchste mögliche wahrscheinlichkeit für einen gewinn gesetzt' (so I simply went for the highest possible probability of winning), which refers to choosing Lottery B because it has a 34% chance of winning, which is slightly higher than Lottery A's 33%. The individual did not mention skewness, distribution shape, or any consideration of how probability is distributed across outcomes. The justification is based solely on the higher probability of winning, not on the skewness of the distribution. Thus, the deliberation process centers on probability magnitude, not on the shape or skewness of the outcome distribution.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual focuses only on the absolute probability of winning (34% vs 33%) and the small difference in prize amounts, not on whether one lottery has a greater concentration of probability on the positive outcome. The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness—specifically, whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes. While Lottery B has a slightly higher probability of winning, the difference is minimal, and the individual does not frame this in terms of distribution shape or skewness. Instead, the justification is based on maximizing the probability of winning, which is a different reasoning principle. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The reasoning process described is consistent with a probability-weighted choice, not a skewness-based one. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

